# In a manner that [[disagree]]s; [[dissent]]ing; [[discordant]].
#* '''1556''', {{w|Thomas More}}; [[w:Ralph Robinson (humanist)|Ralph Robinson]], transl., “The First Book of the Communication of Raphael Hythloday Concerning the Best State of a Commonwealth”, in ''[[w:Utopia (book)|A Frutefull Pleasaunt, [and] Wittie Worke, of the Beste State of a Publique Weale, and of the Newe Yle, Called Vtopia: Written in Latine, by the Right Worthie and Famous Syr Thomas More Knyght, and Translated into Englishe by Raphe Robynson, sometime Fellowe of Corpus Christi College in Oxford, and Nowe by Him at this Seconde Edition Newlie Perused and Corrected, and also with Diuers Notes in the Margent Augmented]]'', 2nd English language edition, London: Imprinted at London: By &#91;{{w|Richard Tottel}} for&#93; Abraham Vele, dwellinge in [[w:St Paul's Cathedral|Pauls churcheyarde]] at the signe of the Lambe, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/606520297 606520297]</small>; reprinted as {{w|Edward Arber}}, editor, ''Utopia. Originally Printed in Latin, 1516. Translated into English by Ralph Robinson, Sometime Fellow of Corpus Christi College, Oxford. His Second and Revised Edition, 1556: Preceded by the Title and Epistle of His First Edition, 1551'', London: Alex, Murray & Son, 30, [[w:Queen Square, London|Queen Square]], W.C., 1 March 1869, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/650389358 650389358]</small>, [https://books.google.com/books?id=uuFLAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA66 pages 65–66]:
#*: Verilye [[of|yf]] all thynges that [[evil|euel]] and [[vitious|vitiouſs]] maners haue caused to [[seem|ſeme]] inconueniente and noughte ſhould be refuſed, as thinges [[unmeet|vnmete]] and reprochefull, then we muſt among Chriſten people [[wink|wynke]] at the moſte parte of al thoſe thinges, whych Chriſt taught vs, and ſo [[streit|ſtreitly]] forbad them to be winked at, [[yet|yat]] thoſe thinges alſo whiche he whiſpered in [[the|ye]] eares of his diſciples he commaunded to be proclaimed in open houſes. And yet ye moſt parte of them is more '''diſſident''' from the maners of the worlde nowe a dayes, then my communication was.
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Journal of the Royal Society of Arts|location=London|publisher={{w|Royal Society of Arts}}|date=25 May 1877|volume=25|page=670|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ouBFAQAAMAAJ&dq=%22dissident%22&pg=PA670#v=onepage&q=%22dissident%22&f=false|oclc=505423162|passage=On flattening either the higher or lower reed separately, by partially pushing in either one of the pulls, '''dissident''' beats instantly arose, which would be made to disappear by partially pushing in the other reed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Kimberley A. Bates|author2=Dean A. Hennessy|chapter=Does Convergence in Regulation Lead to Convergence in Practice? The Case of Dissident Proxy Contests in Canada|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=aLilBAEQDbQC&lpg=PT40&dq=%22dissident%22&pg=PT40#v=onepage&q=%22dissident%22&f=false|editors=Abdul [A.] Rasheed and Toru Yoshikawa|title=The Convergence of Corporate Governance: Promise and Prospects|location=Houndmills, Basingstoke; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Palgrave Macmillan}}|year=2012|isbn=978-1-137-02956-0|passage=In this chapter we look at the question of convergence in corporate governance by evaluating '''dissident''' proxy proposals in Canada. {{...}} '''Dissident''' proxy initiatives are a direct challenge to corporate management and the board. They contain assertions of filers' views of what constitutes legitimate modes of conduct for the corporation, and can be seen as legitimacy contests between filers and corporate management (Bates and Hennessy, 2010). '''Dissident''', or unsolicited, proposals are published along with those management is required to file annually, {{...}}}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A person who formally opposes the current [[political]] [[structure]], the political [[group]] in [[power]], the [[policy|policies]] of the political group in power, or current [[law]]s.
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Claude Monet|magazine=The Speaker|location=London|publisher=Mather & Crowther|date=15 June 1895|volume=11|page=658|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=lhM-AQAAMAAJ&dq=%22a%20dissident%22&pg=PA658#v=onepage&q=%22a%20dissident%22&f=false|oclc=39096282|passage=I once more find myself a '''dissident''', and a '''dissident''' in a very small minority.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Stephen F. Cohen|authorlink=Stephen F. Cohen|author2=Katrina vanden Heuvel|authorlink2=Katrina vanden Heuvel|quotee=Len Karpinsky|chapter=The Autobiography of a ‘Half-Dissident’|title=Voices of Glasnost: Interviews with Gorbachev's Reformers|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|W. W. Norton & Company}}|year=1989|page=280|isbn=978-0-393-02625-2|passage=It is largely the story of a man who fell from being a potential leader of the Soviet Communist Party in the early 1960s to being an outcast by the mid-1970s&nbsp;– a '''dissident''' in the eyes of officialdom, a "half-'''dissident'''" in his own eyes.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=John Horgan|authorlink=John Horgan (political psychologist)|chapter=Here to Stay?|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=7Bf-9w2kvKgC&pg=PT26|title=Divided We Stand: The Strategy and Psychology of Ireland's Dissident Terrorists|location=Oxford; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Oxford University Press}}|year=2013|isbn=978-0-19-977285-8|passage=Before [Martyn] Frampton published his book ''Legion of the Rearguard'', an exhaustive examination of the '''dissidents''', he highlighted sections of it released in a report by the International Center for the Study of Radicalisation (ICSR) at [[w:King's College London|King's College, London]], entitled "The Return of the Militants."}}
# {{lb|en|Christianity}} One who disagrees or dissents; one who separates from the [[established]] [[religion]].
## {{lb|en|Christianity|specifically|historical}} ''Sometimes'' '''{{l|en|Dissident}}''': in the kingdom of {{l|en|Poland}}, the name for [[Christian]]s not part of the [[Roman Catholic Church]].
##* {{quote-journal|en|title=Foreign Advices in May, 1733|editor=“Sylvanus Urban” [pseudonym; {{w|Edward Cave}}]|magazine=[[w:The Gentleman's Magazine|The Gentleman's Magazine: or Monthly Intelligencer]]|location=London|publisher=Printed, and sold at [[w:St John's Gate, Clerkenwell|St John's Gate]], by F. Jeffries in [[w:Ludgate Hill|Ludgate-street]], and most booksellers|month=May|year=1733|volume=III|issue=24|page=272|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=yk87AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA272|oclc=677154342|passage=From ''Warſaw, May 25''. That the Dyet of Convocation had held its laſt Seſſion on the 22d, and then agreed to and ſign'd a general Confederacy in good Order, having firſt Sworn not to Elect a Foreigner as above. The Day of Election was fixt for the 25th of ''Aug''. But a Proteſt was enter'd by the '''''Diſſidents''''', who had been excluded.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=Reflections on the Affairs of the Dissidents in Poland|location=London|publisher=[s.n.]|year=1767|page=7|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=BMIvAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA7|oclc=642456110|passage=The Article which enjoins Peace among the '''Diſſidents''' was ſigned by all the Catholics then present, and ſo are alſo the continual Repetitions of it in all the Pacta Conventa, and in the ſame Terms, to the Death of the late King; whereas the Confederacies of 1717 and 1733 are not signed by the '''Diſſidents''', who were expelled from thence by Force.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|title=VIII. ''Original Pieces, concerning the Present Situation of the Protestants and Greeks in Poland. Wherein are Contained, The Explanation of Their Rights Published by the Court of Russia: The Articles of the Peace of Oliva: The Confederacies of the Dissidents, and the Declarations of the Protestant Courts in Their Favour: The Speeches of the Bishop of Cracovia and the Pope's Nuncio: The Constitutions of the Diet of 1766: And the Articles of the College of the Bishops Allowed to the Dissidents, &c. &c. &c. Translated from the Originals. 8vo. Pr[ice] 2s. 6d.'' Baker.|magazine=[[w:The Critical Review|The Critical Review: Or, Annals of Literature]]|location=London|publisher=Printed for A. Hamilton, in Falcon-Court, Fleet-Street|month=March|year=1768|volume=XXV|pages=206–207|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=I8EPAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA206|oclc=614474270|passage=Theſe Pieces are introduced by a very ſenſible preface, explaining the hardſhips and injuſtice which have been inflicted upon the '''Diſſidents''' of Poland. We there ſee that the '''Diſſidents''' (by whom are meant the proteſtants and the Greeks) had their privileges eſtablished by the fundamental laws paſſed in 1572; and that theſe rights were confirmed by the treaty of Oliva in 1660, which was guarantied by the principal powers of Europe.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=R[obert] G[ordon] Latham|authorlink=Robert Gordon Latham|chapter=Poland from Sigismund II. to the Partition|title=The Nationalities of Europe. [...] In Two Volumes|location=London|publisher=[[w:Virgin Books|Wm. H. Allen & Co.]], 13, Waterloo Place, S.W.|year=1863|volume=I|page=53|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=20QBAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA53|oclc=84435209|passage=A [[Socinian]] was a '''Dissident''', and a member of the Greek Church was a '''Dissident'''; and these '''Dissidents''' agreed to act together. Even a liberal Romanist might be called a '''Dissident'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Brendan [Peter] Simms|authorlink=Brendan Simms|title=Europe: The Struggle for Supremacy, from 1453 to the Present|location=London|publisher=[[w:Penguin Books|Allen Lane]]|year=2013|isbn=978-0-7139-9427-8|passage=The rest, about half a million Russian Orthodox and about the same number of Protestants, were known as ‘'''dissidents'''’; the huge Jewish community defied classification.}}

